WEBVTT

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.130
A fellow knitter, Mary, was lamenting that
the Jaggerspun of Maine Mill was closing.

00:00:06.163 --> 00:00:09.930
She said, I love their Superlamb 4/8.

00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:12.410
What can I substitute?

00:00:12.443 --> 00:00:17.890
Now, the best way to find a similar yarn
is to first look for the fiber you like.

00:00:17.923 --> 00:00:23.000
The Superlamb was 100%
superwash wool on cones.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.810
Sadly, there are very few choices for us
here in the US for any yarn on cones.

00:00:28.843 --> 00:00:32.720
Let's take a look at yarn
on balls or hanks.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.650
Yarn is often classified by the Craft Yarn
Council numbering system, one through six.

00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:44.850
But here are two ways
that can be more accurate.

00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:48.640
First, look at yards per pound.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:56.640
Using the Knitted Now Vintage Yarn Index,
we can see that the Superlamb had

00:00:56.673 --> 00:00:59.890
approximately 1120 yards per pound.

00:00:59.923 --> 00:01:05.610
Our Yarn Index is a listing of vintage
yarns that you can use for reference,

00:01:05.643 --> 00:01:08.570
especially when using vintage patterns.

00:01:08.603 --> 00:01:12.320
Looking at the Knitpicks Wool
of the Andes Superwash, it has

00:01:12.353 --> 00:01:17.330
approximately 110 yards per 50 grams.

00:01:17.363 --> 00:01:21.690
Using the Knit It Now Yards per Pound
Tool, we can calculate that

00:01:21.723 --> 00:01:29.090
110 yards in a 50 gram ball is
approximately 997 yards per pound.

00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:35.240
Comparing a 1120 to 997,
we can tell that the Knit Picks yarn will

00:01:35.273 --> 00:01:38.440
be heavier than what she was using,
and it won't give her the same

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:41.290
hand or drape she's used to.

00:01:41.323 --> 00:01:43.010
Let's look at another yarn.

00:01:43.043 --> 00:01:46.450
Here's an old standby,
the Lion brand Wool Ease.

00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:52.210
It's listed as having
180 meters in 85 grams.

00:01:52.243 --> 00:01:57.730
The tool tells us that this is
approximately 1,050 yards per pound.

00:01:57.763 --> 00:02:02.840
Now, this may knit up closer
to what Mary had used in the past.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.970
The second way to compare yarns
is to look at the suggested gauge.

00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:14.000
The Wool of the Andes is listed as 18
to 20 stitches to 4 inches, and the Wool

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.650
Ease is 18 stitches to 4 inches.

00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:22.560
In addition to the yards per pound,
comparing these gauges to what she used

00:02:22.593 --> 00:02:28.400
with her original yarn will give her
even more information to compare.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:35.007
Using yards per pound and the gauge is much
more accurate way of comparing yarn than

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.520
the Craft Yarn Council numbering system.

